--TEST--
a `net.connect` grant does not reach an address `rule:security/net-address-policy` denies, and the refusal names the range — `rule:http-server/allow-url-pins-the-address`, `rule:security/net-address-policy` and `rule:security/the-policy-lives-in-the-capability`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Both hosts below are granted by name in `nvs.toml`, and both are still
// refused. That is § 3's point: the grant answers "may this program talk to
// that name", and the address policy answers "is that address one no
// deployment means to expose", and the second question is asked of the
// resolved address rather than of the name. A policy applied to the hostname
// would be re-resolved out from under itself.
try {
    var $metadata = Core\Http::allowUrl("http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/");
    echo "accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The private ranges are denied on the same reading, and for the reason the
// link-local one above is called out separately: the cloud metadata endpoint
// is the canonical target, and its omission is catastrophic rather than merely
// regrettable.
try {
    var $internal = Core\Http::allowUrl("http://10.0.0.7/admin");
    echo "accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The grant is as wide as an operator can write it for these two names, and it
# still buys nothing: `rule:security/the-policy-lives-in-the-capability` puts the address policy in the capability,
# where it applies to every outbound connection rather than to whichever client
# remembered to check. `net.internal` is § 3's other half and is absent here, so
# these two addresses are refused by the table with nothing excepted from it.
[capabilities.net]
connect = ["169.254.169.254", "10.0.0.7"]
--EXPECT--
Core\Http::allowUrl refuses 169.254.169.254: it is link-local (169.254.0.0/16), which `net.internal` does not except
Core\Http::allowUrl refuses 10.0.0.7: it is private (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16), which `net.internal` does not except
